  "query_preview": "..High pressure aloft remains anchored west of the \nsouthern Baja Peninsula with Arizona on the front side of the ridge. \nThis will result in above normal temperatures and periods of high \nclouds through Monday. High temperatures will be 7-12 degrees above \nnormal through this period, with temperatures peaking on Sunday. \nHighs on Sunday will be in the lower to mid 70s in the valleys of \nGraham, Cochise and Santa Cruz counties and in the mid 70s to lower \n80s in the lower elevations of Pinal and Pima counties.  \n\nThe ridge begins to flatten out on Monday as one system moves into \nthe Pacific Northwest, with shortwave ridging across southeast \nArizona on Tuesday in advance of another storm system approaching \nthe southern California coast. High temperatures lower to 3-7 \ndegrees above normal during the day Tuesday with lowering heights \nand increasing cloud cover. Southwesterly flow ahead of this \napproaching system will result in breezy to locally windy south to \nsouthwest winds of 15-30 mph and gusts up to 45 mph. This could \nresult in some areas of blowing dust Tuesday afternoon.  \n\nThe ensembles, deterministic GFS/ECMWF and clusters are all in good \nagreement with the track and overall timing of this system in the \nlate Tuesday through midday Thursday time frame. The chance of \nprecipitation starts late afternoon Tuesday to the west of Tucson, \nspreading across the entire forecast area Tuesday night and \nWednesday, before winding down from west to east late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday morning. With the track of the upper low moving \nfrom west to east across south central Arizona, there will be strong \nforcing/lift/dynamics and the possibility of embedded thunderstorms, \nalong with widespread valley rain and high elevation snow.   \n\nStorm total rainfall amounts over 24-30 hours (late Tuesday through \nmidday Thursday) continue to trend upward, with 0.80-1.5 inches in \nthe valleys and 1.25-1.80 inches in the mountains. Expect even \nhigher amounts with any convection/thunderstorms. In fact, WPC has a \nSlight Risk of excessive rainfall generally from Tucson west on Day \n4 and from Tucson eastward for Day 5. Snow levels remain high, \ngenerally above 8500 feet through Wednesday morning, lowering to \n7500 feet Wednesday evening. Snowfall amounts above 7500 feet will \nbe 1-4 inches in the Catalina Mountains, 4-8 inches in the Pinaleno \nMountains/Mount Graham and 5-10 inches for the White Mountains of \nnorthern Greenlee County. High temperatures fall to 5-8 degrees \nbelow normal Wednesday and Thursday of next week with the passage of \nthis system. Highs will be in the mid 50s to lower 60s across valley \nlocations in Graham, Cochise and Santa Cruz counties, and in the \nlower to mid 60s across the lower elevations of Pinal and Pima \ncounties.\n\nBehind the storm system, weak shortwave ridging takes place late \nThursday and Friday. High temperatures will warm slowly, but still \nremain 3-5 degrees below normal into Saturday. The models then show \nthe potential for a couple of systems late next weekend into early \nthe following week. At this time, there are differences between the \nmodels on the timing/track of these systems and therefore the \nimpacts to southeast Arizona.",
